    "text": "Ping is a network diagnostic tool used to test connectivity between your device and a target (website/server/IP). Here's a quick guide:\n\n### Basic Usage\n```bash\nping [target]\n```\nExamples:\n- `ping google.com` (test connection to Google)\n- `ping 8.8.8.8` (test Google's public DNS)\n- `ping 192.168.1.1` (test local network/router)\n\n### Common Options\n| Option | Purpose |\n|--------|---------|\n| `-t` | Continuous ping (Windows: Ctrl+C to stop; Linux/macOS: auto-stops after time) |\n| `-c [count]` | Send specific # of packets (Linux/macOS, e.g., `ping -c 4 google.com`) |\n| `-n [count]` | Send specific # of packets (Windows) |\n| `-a` | Resolve IPs to hostnames (Windows) |\n| `-s [size]` | Set packet size (e.g., `ping -s 1000 google.com`) |\n| `-i [interval]` | Wait interval seconds between pings (Linux/macOS) |\n\n### What to Look For in Results\n- **Reply from:** Target is reachable\n- **Time:** Latency (lower = faster)\n- **TTL (Time to Live):** Hops remaining before packet expires (OS detection hint)\n- **Packet loss:** 0% = perfect connection\n- **Request timed out:** Target unreachable/blocked\n\n### Troubleshooting Tips\n1. Fail? Try pinging your router (gateway IP) first\n2. Then try pinging `8.8.8.8` (tests external connectivity)\n3. Then try a domain name (tests DNS)\n\nWould you like help interpreting specific ping results or troubleshooting a network issue?",
